<div class="intro">

<p>I am an ML Engineer on the AI Infrastructure team at <a href="https://10xengineers.ai">10xEngineers</a> and a Teaching Fellow at <a href="https://lums.edu.pk">LUMS</a>, where I completed my BS in Electrical Engineering, advised on my final year project by Dr. Muhammad Tahir.</p>

<p>I work on efficient machine learning: quantization, model compression, and distributed training, aimed at language models that are private, fast, and cheap to run. Memory bandwidth and energy dominate the cost of a deployed model, and transferring data off the device dominates its privacy risk. I therefore work close to the software stack, where both are determined.</p>

<p>I develop <a href="/projects/#quantx"><strong>QuantX</strong></a>, a <strong>hardware-aware quantization</strong> framework for LLM and VLM inference. Most quantization methods assume an idealised numeric abstraction that deployed hardware does not provide: the supported data formats and available kernels constrain what the algorithm can do, and methods tuned in the abstract often fail to transfer. I invert that order and derive the algorithm from what the stack implements, rather than requiring the stack to support a scheme fixed in advance. Two manuscripts on this <strong>hardware–software co-design</strong> are in preparation, one of them <a href="/publications/">under review</a>.</p>

<p>Earlier in my BS, my research was on <a href="/projects/"><strong>federated learning</strong></a>. My thesis looked at sharpness-aware minimization under non-IID clients: each client perturbs against its own loss surface, so SAM ends up flattening the wrong objective. I studied perturbations aimed at global rather than local sharpness.</p>

<p>Separately, in another project, I looked at replacing parameter averaging altogether. Averaging permutation-invariant networks is unsound when clients disagree, so we distilled functional behaviour instead, matching logits from client teachers into a rotating student that uses only its own local data.</p>

<p>I am applying to <strong>MS / PhD</strong> programs for <strong>Fall 2027</strong> in efficient machine learning and ML systems. Contact me at <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">{{ site.email }}</a>.</p>

